---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';
import { getApps } from '../lib/catalog';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const apps = getApps();
const count = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const appUrl = (slug: string) => `${base}apps/${slug}/`;
const app = (slug: string) => {
  const found = apps.find((entry) => entry.slug === slug);
  if (!found) throw new Error(`how-we-verify: no app record "${slug}"`);
  return found;
};
const restricted = apps.filter(
  (entry) => entry.sourceAvailability === 'source-available',
);
const host = (entry: (typeof apps)[number]) => new URL(entry.download).hostname;
const fromGithub = apps.filter((entry) => host(entry) === 'github.com').length;
const fromAppStore = apps.filter(
  (entry) => host(entry) === 'apps.apple.com',
).length;
const fromElsewhere = apps.length - fromGithub - fromAppStore;
const dates = apps.map((entry) => entry.verified).sort();
const formatDate = (iso: string) =>
  new Date(`${iso}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
    day: 'numeric',
    month: 'long',
    year: 'numeric',
    timeZone: 'UTC',
  });
// Read from GitHub's API (licenseInfo.spdxId) on 2026-11-06; method in docs/VERIFICATION.md.
const examples = [
  {
    app: app('rectangle'),
    file: 'MIT, with the copyright notice of Spectacle, the app it is based on',
  },
  {
    app: app('coteditor'),
    file: 'Apache-2.0 for the source code, CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 for the image resources',
  },
  {
    app: app('handbrake'),
    file: 'GPLv2 for compiled builds; some files are under GPLv2+, LGPLv2.1+ or BSD-style terms',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`How we verify licenses and downloads — ${site.name}`}
  description={`How ${site.name} checks each Mac app: the license file read by hand, a separate source-available label, official downloads only and a dated check.`}
  type="WebPage"
  section="about/"
>
  <article class="about-page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>How We Verify</h1>
      <p>
        Every app on {site.name} passed the same checks before it was listed.
        This page explains them, with examples from the catalog.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section>
      <h2>We read the license file.</h2>
      <p>
        Before an app is listed, we open its license file and read it. We don’t
        take the license GitHub detects, a directory’s label or a README badge.
        The listing names the license, links the exact file we read, and keeps a
        copy of its text.
      </p>
      <p>
        An app stays out if it has no license file, if its README and license
        file disagree, or if its license is not on our reviewed list.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>GitHub’s license field is a hint.</h2>
      <p>
        GitHub matches a repository’s license file against known licenses. When
        it can’t name a match, it reports <code>NOASSERTION</code>. On November
        6, 2026, GitHub’s API named no license for 112 of the apps listed here:
        104 showed <code>NOASSERTION</code> and 8 showed none. The files
        themselves are clear:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">App</th>
            <th scope="col">GitHub says</th>
            <th scope="col">The license file says</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {examples.map(({ app, file }) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href={appUrl(app.slug)}>{app.name}</a>
              </th>
              <td>
                <code>NOASSERTION</code>
              </td>
              <td>
                <a href={app.licenseUrl}>{file}</a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The 14 source-available apps listed on that date were all among them. We
        never turn a missing match into a guessed license: we read the file, or
        the app stays out.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Source available is a separate label.</h2>
      <p>
        Some apps publish their code under terms that limit what you can do with
        it: no selling, no commercial use, no competing products, or a wait
        before an open license applies. These terms don’t meet the{' '}
        <a href="https://opensource.org/osd">Open Source Definition</a>, so we
        label these apps Source available. {restricted.length} apps carry the
        label, among them{' '}
        <a href={appUrl('pearcleaner')}>{app('pearcleaner').name}</a>{' '}
        (Apache-2.0 with Commons Clause),{' '}
        <a href={appUrl('mos')}>{app('mos').name}</a> (CC BY-NC 4.0),{' '}
        <a href={appUrl('gitbutler')}>{app('gitbutler').name}</a> (FSL-1.1-MIT)
        and <a href={appUrl('mac-mouse-fix')}>{app('mac-mouse-fix').name}</a>{' '}
        (its own license).
      </p>
      <p>
        The label is shown in orange under the Get button, and each of these app
        pages explains the restriction. They are never counted as open source.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={`${base}search/?source=source-available`}>
          Browse the source-available apps
        </a>{' '}
        or <a href={`${base}search/?source=open-source`}>only open source</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Downloads come from the project.</h2>
      <p>
        The Get button links to the download the project itself publishes. Of
        the apps listed today, {count(fromGithub)} link to GitHub,{' '}
        {count(fromAppStore)} to the Mac App Store, and {count(fromElsewhere)}{' '}
        to the download page the project points to, usually its own website. We
        don’t host, mirror or repackage installers, and we don’t link to
        download sites. A Homebrew command is shown only when the cask downloads
        from the app’s own repository or website.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also leave out apps whose official download is not built from the
        open code. Rockxy and Tracexy publish AGPL source, but their official
        disk images ship under a proprietary license.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Every listing has a check date.</h2>
      <p>
        Each app page shows, under Verified, the date we last checked its
        license, source and download. The current listings were checked between{' '}
        {formatDate(dates[0]!)} and {formatDate(dates.at(-1)!)}. If a project
        changes its license, we review the listing again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The date records our review. It is not a security audit, and we don’t
        install every app.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Where the apps come from.</h2>
      <p>
        We started from 10 <a href={`${base}about/`}>community lists</a> of Mac
        apps. On November 2, 2026, they linked to 1,975 GitHub repositories.
        Homebrew’s install data added 382 more leads. A lead is only listed
        after it passes the checks above. More than 1,000 apps are listed so
        far.
      </p>
      <p>
        The{' '}
        <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/main/docs/RESEARCH.md`}>
          research notes
        </a>{' '}
        list every source and every app we left out, with the reason.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
      <p>
        Every listing is a folder in a public repository. Report a wrong
        license, a broken or unofficial download or a moved repository with the
        listing problem form, or open a pull request. Corrections from an app’s
        maintainers are applied first.
      </p>
      <a
        class="button"
        href={`${site.repository}/issues/new?template=listing-problem.yml`}
      >
        Report a Listing Problem <ArrowUpRight size={16} />
      </a>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
